['Here's a simple C code implementation of the described requirements. This program takes two command-line arguments: the first is used as an index to access the second argument, which is expected to be a string representation of an integer. The atoi function converts this string to an integer.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int atoi(const char *str) {
    int result = 0;
    while (*str != '\0') {
        result = result * 10 + (*str - '0');
        str++;
    }
    return result;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc < 3) {
        printf("Usage: %s index string\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int index = atoi(argv[1]);
    if (index < 1 || index > argc - 1) {
        printf("Invalid index\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Argument %d: %s\n", index, argv[index]);

    return 0;
}
```

This program will compile and run on any system with a standard C library (e.g., gcc or clang). To run this code, save it in a file named `my_program.c`, then open a terminal, navigate to the directory containing the file, and type:

```sh
gcc -o my_program my_program.c
./my_program 2 arg2
```

Replace "arg2" with your desired string argument at index 2, and change the number in the command as needed to specify a different index for the argument you want to access.']

[' Here is a simple C code implementation that mimics the behavior of `atoi()` and handles command line arguments. This code takes in the number of arguments (argc) and the array of strings containing the arguments (argv). It starts processing from the second argument since the first one (argv[0]) is the name of the program.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int atoi_and_process(const char* str) {
    int result = 0;
    int negative = 0;

    // Check for the sign
    if (*str == '-') {
        negative = 1;
        str++;
    }

    while (*str >= '0' && *str <= '9') {
        result = result * 10 + (*str - '0');
        str++;
    }

    // Set the sign of the result if necessary
    if (negative)
        result *= -1;

    return result;
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    int sum = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i) {
        // Skip non-numeric arguments
        if (!isdigit(*argv[i][0])) continue;

        // Convert argument to integer and add to the sum
        sum += atoi_and_process(argv[i]);
    }

    printf("The total is: %d\n", sum);

    return 0;
}
```

When you run this code as `./a.out foo 1 2 3 4`, it will output the total of `10`. Keep in mind that this implementation does not follow strict adherence to the atoi() function as there is no error checking for invalid inputs or overflow. It also processes only the numeric arguments passed through command line, skipping non-numeric ones.']

